    "updated_at": "2026-10-15T10:27:21Z",
    "body": "The suggested change should keep streamed scripts behaving like document parsing, even when streaming into a disconnected target:\n\n> The document parser already behaves the way the proposed #12758 change would, when a parser-inserted script is disconnected at its end tag.\n>\n>I tested `<div id=c><script>c.remove()</script><script src=slow.js></script><script src=fast.js></script><script>…</script></div>`, so the parser inserts the scripts into the detached `div`, with `slow.js` delayed by 5 s. None of the scripts run at parse time. While detached, `script.async` is `true` for all three. After appending the `div` back to the document, they run `inline`, `fast`, `slow`. Same result in Chrome Canary 158, Firefox Nightly 160, and Safari TP.\n>\n>This matches the spec: at the end tag, [prepare the script element](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#prepare-the-script-element) has a parser document and no `async` attribute, so it sets force async to true, then returns at \"If el is not connected, then return\". On insertion, the script isn't parser-inserted and force async is true, so it runs async.\n>\n> So with the proposed change, `createContextualFragment()`, `setHTMLUnsafe()` with `runScripts`, and streaming into a disconnected target all behave like document-parser scripts that were disconnected when prepared.\n",
